From: mscharged-port
Subject: Give native GL resource pools room for native header sizes
Upstream-base: 9046e67aa2077a7eb4ed6e28c9df3c1d3b368f84
Classification: native data-size adaptation; apply after 0688.

Records loaded from disc keep their Wii header quotas (0180, 0238), but
models, packets and material parameters built or cloned at run time are
allocated at their native sizes (a packet is 80 bytes instead of 48). That
can push a pool's MEM1 part past its Wii budget: loading Bowser's Striker
Challenge reached 1,312,560 of the InGame pool's 1,310,720 bytes and stopped
in GLXResourcePool::Allocate's out-of-memory break while creating the crowd
impostor targets. Natively every pool's MEM1 part gets a quarter more room.
The game never reads pool sizes for decisions (only a debug print and a
discarded query), so behaviour is unchanged.

diff --git a/src/NL/glx/glxMemory.cpp b/src/NL/glx/glxMemory.cpp
index 59fbfa9..ebf1e9a 100644
--- a/src/NL/glx/glxMemory.cpp
+++ b/src/NL/glx/glxMemory.cpp
@@ -463,6 +463,11 @@ GLResourcePool* glplatCreateResourcePool(
         int pool = GetMemoryPoolIndex(requirements[i].mType);
         sizes[pool] += requirements[i].mSize;
     }
+#if defined(MSCHARGED_NATIVE)
+    // Models, packets and material parameters that are built or cloned at run
+    // time take their larger native sizes from the MEM1 part; give it room.
+    sizes[0] += sizes[0] / 4;
+#endif
 
     return new (8, false)
         GLXResourcePool(sizes, name);
